## Supporting Information
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of crystalline nano-porous materials in which inorganic building units are connected by organic links via coordination bonding. Over the past few years, material chemists have used MOFs for their potential applications mainly in gas storage, gas separation and catalysis.2-6 Recently, there is a growing interest in studying MOFs as a newtype semiconducting materials. MOFs with conductivity higher than $0.1\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ have been achieved by Allendorf, Dincă, Nishihara and others.7-11 Talin and his coworkers presented the first example of a thermoelectric effect in $\mathrm{TCNQ}@\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ with large and positive Seebeck coefficient.12 Grzybowski, Li and Liu reported the potential  storage of electrical information in MOFbased memory devices.13, 14 Dincă et al. used a conductive MOF, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , to fabricate chemiresistive sensor devices.15 These pioneering scientific reports have set the foundations for the application of MOFs within the domain of electronic devices.
Field-effect transistors (FETs) are three-terminal semiconductor devices where a voltage related to the “gate” terminal controls the electric current in the channel between the “source” and the “drain” terminals. FETs play critical roles in modern electronic applications.16 For example, they are well known as the fundamental building blocks of the central processing unit (CPU) in computers. More recently, their versatile features have expanded their applications to state-of-the-art technologies including flat panel displays, memory devices, sensors, nanofluidic transistors etc.17-20 Moreover, a FET presents a powerful way for the investigation of the intrinsic electronic properties of semiconducting materials. The charge carrier type, carrier mobility and carrier concentration, can be revealed by taking the semiconductor as the channel material in the device.21 The features of conductive MOFs, such as long range crystallinity, rich and designable structure, modulatable pathways for charge transport, tunable electronic band structure by ligand modification and metal selection, ultra-light and low density, and easy solution processability for membrane preparation make them very attractive to be used as active channel materials in FETs.22 On the other hand, FETs with porous channel can greatly extend their applications on voltage-gated ion channels / microfluidic chips and vastly enhance the sensitivity of FET-based gas / ion sensors.23-26 FETs with the meso- or macroporous channels (ranging from several nanometers to hundreds of nanometers) have been already reported.27,28 However they normally present large size-distributed and disorderedly-arranged pores. Recently, FETs based on the materials with ordered pores, e.g. $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{N}{-}h2\mathrm{D}$ crystal and covalent organic framework (COF), have emerged.29,30 Comparatively, conductive MOF-based materials possessing pores of narrow size-distribution, tunable structure and good electrical properties, are expected to be another promising material for porous-FET.31, 32 To our knowledge, MOFbased FETs have never been reported.
In this work, microporous FET devices fabricated by using a semiconductive MOF as the active channel are constructed. We highlight the preparation and the characterization of a high-quality MOF membrane, together with its fabrication and structure, as well as the detailed characterization of the derived porous FETs. Notably, the FETs show typical p-type depletion-mode with the best on / off ratio of more than $\overline{{10^{3}}}$ and the field-effect charge mobility comparable with the best values obtained from the devices based on solution-processed organic semiconductors or inorganic semiconductors.33-36
Among reported semiconductive MOFs, $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , which consists of nickel ions and organic HITP components $(\mathrm{HITP}{=}2,3,6,7,10,1$ 1-hexaiminotriphenylenesemiquinonate), has drawn great attention for its interesting graphene-like honeycomb porous structure and excellent electronic conductivity (Figure 1a). $\mathrm{\bfNi}_{3}(\mathrm{\bfHITP})_{2}$ membrane can be prepared by directly placing a quartz substrate inside the reaction vessel where the MOF is formed.37 However, the obtained film is not compact and smooth. The challenge to prepare high-quality film has restricted the application of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ in semiconductor devices. Herein, we demonstrate an air / liquid interfacial method to prepare an ultrasmooth and compact $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane for FET device (for details, see the Supporting Information). By heating the aqua solution of HATP·6HCl $_{\mathrm{RATP=}2,3,6,7,10,11}$ - hexaaminotriphenylene), $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and trimethylamine to $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Scheme S1), $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ coordinated to in situ-generated HITP and selfassembled to form $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ nanoparticles in air / liquid interface. The hydrophobic $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ nanoparticles float on the water surface which acts as a “smooth substrate”, and consequently the nanoparticles closely packed to form a nanometer-thick uniform layer due to the interface-confining effect (Figure S1).38,39 After the air $/$ liquid interface was fully covered with this $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ layer, the MOF membrane probably formed through the process that organic HITP components and nickel ions in the solution continuously assembled onto the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ layer at the MOF / solution interface. The formation of the membrane can be observed even by the naked eye at the interface. The color of the membrane became deeper when it grew thicker and it finally turned dark blue-violet (Figure 1b). The thickness of the membrane positively relates to the reaction time and a membrane with ${\sim}100\mathrm{nm}$ thickness was obtained after reaction for $3\mathrm{min}$ .
In the structure of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , the tritopic HITP moieties are connected by the square-planar $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ centers to form a twodimensional (2D) graphene-like honeycomb porous framework with 6-fold symmetry (Figure 1a). In the lattice packing, two neighboring graphene-like layers stack in AB mode with an interval of $3.5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and result in 1D channels with open window size around $1.4~\mathrm{nm}$ . Figure S2 depicts the nitrogen sorption isotherms measured at $77\mathrm{K}$ , which shows significant gas uptake and release during adsorption and desorption processes. This observation indicates the permanent porosity in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . Specific surface area and pore volume values from a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) were calculated as $625~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.511~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}.$ , respectively, comparable with values from other 2D crystalline porous materials.40,41 To study the structure of the prepared $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane, the XRD patterns were further collected using two different scattering geometries: in-plane XRD and out-of-plane XRD, as shown in Figure S3. The prominent peaks in both in-plan and outof-plane XRD patterns at $2\theta=4.7^{\circ}$ , $9.5^{\circ}:$ , $12.6^{\circ}$ , $16.5^{\circ}$ and $27.3^{\circ}$ are all present, which is consistent with the crystal structure of the previously reported $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}.$ The in-plane and out-of-plane patterns have almost identical peaks implying the prepared membrane has no preferred orientation.
$\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane was transferred onto a silicon wafer to evaluate its quality. Scanning electron microscope (SEM) image shows that the bottom-surface of the prepared membrane in the air / liquid surface is relative rough (Figure S4). However, the topsurface of the same membrane is ultra-flat, compact and uniform in a large scale without any steps or cracks (Figure 1c). Atomic force microscopy (AFM) was used to further confirm the average surface roughness (RMS) of the top-surface. As shown in Figure 1d, the top-surface of the membrane is pinhole-less and its RMS is only ${\sim}1.05~\mathrm{nm}$ in $3\times3~\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ . Even in the area as large as $20\times$ $20\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ , the average RMS of 9 randomly selected areas is only ${\sim}1.43~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S5). The high-quality of the top-surface of the solution-prepared membrane is comparable to those prepared by the vacuum deposition technique, being good enough for FET device fabrication.
It is critical to know the composition of the thin layer (top surface of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane) close to the gate dielectric which is the majority of conduction occurs in a FET device. The XRD on the top surface of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane (Figure S3) shows no typical peaks of Ni, NiO and $\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}$ (Figure S6). Meanwhile, the results from X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurement (Figure S7) also reveal that Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ on the top surface of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane is located at $855.5~\mathrm{eV}$ and has very weak broad satellite peak which is the same as that of pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ crystallite in the previous report,37,42 while significant different from these of Ni $852.6~\mathrm{eV})$ , NiO $(853.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}$ (855.2 eV) 43 (as analyzed in Supporting Information). These observations rule out the presence of impurities of Ni species (e.g. Ni metal, NiO and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2})$ at the thin layer close to $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ layer.
As a class of newly emerging porous conducting material, semiconductive MOFs are very attractive for use as active layers in FET devices to open their applications in electronic device fields and to develop new avenues for further revealing their electronic properties. Notably, a perfect semiconductor / dielectric interface is very important and necessary for a FET device.44 The smooth and dense surface of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane prepared in this work affords a great opportunity to prepare porous MOF-FET devices. As shown in Figure 2, we can easily transfer the as-prepared membrane to a $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ / Si wafer substrate in a top-surface of membrane / $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ mode by a simple stamping process. The smooth topsurface of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane directly contacts with $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ / Si wafer and creates a high-quality interface between the MOF semiconductor and the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ dielectric. After deposited
patterned Au electrodes, porous FETs based on the MOF membrane were fabricated. Figures 2 and S8 show the prepared device possesses a bottom-gate top-contact device geometry with 105 nm-thick membrane as the active channel material, $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ -thick Au thin film as the source / drain electrodes and heavily doped ptype Si as the gate electrode. The device has a channel length of $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and a width of $1000\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .
Figure 3a shows the output characteristics of the porous FET device. The drain-source current $\left(I_{\mathrm{ds}}\right)$ versus drain-source voltage $(V_{\mathrm{ds}})$ curves of the device have good linear regimes, indicating good ohmic contact between Au electrodes and the porous $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ channel, which is favored for charge injection or exhaustion. Meanwhile, $\ensuremath{I_{\mathrm{ds}}}$ versus $V_{\mathrm{ds}}$ curves show a gradual slope increase by reducing the gate-source voltage $(V_{\mathrm{gs}})$ from $20~\mathrm{V}$ to - $20~\mathrm{V}$ at $-10{\mathrm{~V~}}$ steps, which indicates the carrier concentration in the porous channel can be effective modulated by altering $V_{\mathrm{{gs}}}$ . The conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ in a film form is $40\ \mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature,37 but other electronic features, such as charge carrier type and charge mobility have not been revealed. Figure 3b shows the transfer curves $\mathrm{\Delta}I_{\mathrm{ds}}$ versus $V_{\mathrm{gs}},$ $V_{\mathrm{ds}}=-1~\mathrm{V}.$ ) of the porous FET device. The channel current gradually increases with reducing the $V_{\mathrm{gs}}$ , which confirms the observation in the output curves and also demonstrates that the device is a typical $\mathfrak{p}$ -type transistor where the application of positive gate bias repels the carriers and the negative gate bias accumulates the carriers. The $\mathfrak{p}$ -type behavior indicates $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is a semiconductor with holes as the majority carriers. The threshold voltage is ${\sim}1.1\mathrm{~V~}$ extracted from linear fit of $V_{\mathrm{gs}}$ versus $-I_{\mathrm{ds}}$ (Figure S9), which indicates that the device is a depletion-mode FET. Figure 3b also shows the current on / off ratio in this device is approximately $2\times10^{3}$ .
Simultaneously, five other devices at the same $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si}$ substrate were also characterized (see Figure S10, from device 2 to device 6), and the current on / off ratio in these devices are between 350 and 2000 (Figure S11). The subthreshold swing (SS) value of this $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ -based FET is $2.4\mathrm{~V~},$ / decade. The $S S$ value in a FET depends on the number of defects in the interface between the active layer and the insulator layer.45 The interface defect density $(N_{\mathrm{t}})$ is $8.2{\times}10^{12}\ /\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ in this device, which indicates a relatively low defect density at the interface between $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ channel layer and $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ gate dielectric. The high-quality interface in the porous FET is ascribed to the smooth surface of the free-standing $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane. The calculated hole mobility is $\sim48.6$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{V}\cdot\mathrm{:}$ s in this device and are $38{\pm}8~\mathrm{cm^{2}/V\cdot s}$ in five other devices (Figure S12), indicating excellent hole charge transport properties of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane. The value surpasses that of most organic semiconductors and is even comparable to those of inorganic oxide semiconductors.46-48 The reason for this high mobility might be elucidated from the point of view of the molecular structure of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , where $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ coordinates to radical $o$ - semiquinonate HITP to form an extended fully charge-delocalized layer in the $a b$ plane of the crystal structure.37 The $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{isq})_{2}$ $\mathrm{isq}=$ $o$ -diiminobenzosemiquinonate) part in the structure has been reported to show a rich electrochemical activity of reversible oxidation and reduction.49 Therefore, a charge carrier can transport within the layer smoothly. On the other hand, along $c$ axis of the structure, above-mentioned conjugated layers overlap with the interval of only $3.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is short enough to create sufficient orbital overlap between adjacent layers through $\pi-\pi$ interaction.50 Thus, both the so-called “through-bond” (within 2D layer) and “through-space” channels (among layers) for charge transport can be observed in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and may synergistically enhance the charge mobility in the membrane.50 Notably, there is no transistor characteristic saturation was observed in our devices, and the on/off ratios of transfer curves were dramatically reduced at varied $V_{\mathrm{ds}}$ (Figure S13). The reason for this behavior may be attributed to the high conductivity of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ membrane. This phenomenon is common in the FETs made from high conductive channel materials like graphene and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BHT.51,52.
In conclusion, a free-standing semiconductive porous membrane has been successfully prepared with a compact and smooth surface by an air $/$ liquid interfacial self-assembly process. After transferring it to a $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ / Si substrate, the first microporous FET with a MOF as the active channel material was demonstrated. The porous channel may greatly extend the applications of FETs to the fields that require channel material having large surface area and penetrability, such as FET-based sensors, voltage-gated ion channels and microfluidic chips. The prepared FET shows distinguishable on and off states as well as a remarkably high large charge mobility. Other electronic features of the MOF material, such as charge carrier type and interface defect density, were also revealed for the first time due to the FET structure. Our results provide a step forward for both conductive MOF membrane and MOF-based devices. Moreover, the merits of MOFs like variable and designable structure, regular pore and large surface area, tunable band structure by chemical modification give us infinite imagination of its application on electronic devices in future.
